language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ frog_circle: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Cards numbered 1 to n are arranged in a circle. A frog jumps on card 1, then jumps to the card 1
$ place clockwise around the circle. Then, from card k, he jumps directly to the card k places
$ clockwise around the circle. The frog continues jumping in this way forever.
$
$ Find an arrangement of cards that allows the frog to jump on every card starting from card 1.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `frog_circle.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/frog_circle.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `frog_circle_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py frog_circle.json
$   savilerow model.eprime frog_circle_1.param -run-solver

$ number of cards
given n : int
$ x[i]: the card at position i of the circle (positions 1 to n, clockwise); card 1 is at position 1
find x : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(1..n)
$ p[k]: the position the frog is on after k-1 jumps (the frog starts on card 1, at position 1)
find p : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(1..n)

such that
$ the cards are the numbers 1 to n, each once
allDiff(x),
$ the frog starts on card 1; rotating the circle gives the same arrangement, so card 1 is put at position 1 (symmetry break)
x[1] = 1,
p[1] = 1,
$ the frog lands on every position once
allDiff(p),
$ from the card it is on, the frog jumps that many places clockwise around the circle (wrapping round)
forAll k : int(1..n-1) . p[k+1] = p[k] + x[p[k]] - n * toInt(p[k] + x[p[k]] > n)
